NNAAMMEE
     iiffmmccssttaatt — dump multicast group management statistics per interface

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     iiffmmccssttaatt [--ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e] [--ff _a_d_d_r_e_s_s_-_f_a_m_i_l_y] [--vv] [--KK] [--MM _c_o_r_e]
              [--NN _s_y_s_t_e_m]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iiffmmccssttaatt command dumps multicast group information from the kernel.

     The following options are supported:

     --ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
                 specifies the interface to be displayed.

     --ff _a_d_d_r_e_s_s_-_f_a_m_i_l_y
                 specifies the address family to be displayed; _i_n_e_t, _i_n_e_t_6 and
                 _l_i_n_k are supported.

     --vv          specifies that link-layer memberships should be printed; they
                 are suppressed by default.  It may not be specified for --ff
                 _l_i_n_k.  Source lists for each group will also be printed.

                 If specified twice, and kvm(3) is in use, the control plane
                 timers for each interface and the source list counters for
                 each group will also be printed.

     The following options are only available if iiffmmccssttaatt has been built with
     support for kvm(3):

     --KK          attempts to use kvm(3) to retrieve the multicast group infor‐
                 mation.

     --MM _c_o_r_e     extracts values associated with the name list from the speci‐
                 fied core, instead of the default _/_d_e_v_/_k_m_e_m.

     --NN _s_y_s_t_e_m   extracts the name list from the specified kernel instead of
                 the default, which is the kernel image the system has booted
                 from.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     iiffmmccssttaatt will always print the embedded scope IDs of IPv6 multicast group
     memberships.  This is because memberships are always scoped to an inter‐
     face.

     When run with the --vv option, iiffmmccssttaatt may print multicast MAC addresses
     twice if they are referenced by a layer 3 protocol.

     When run with kvm(3) support, the names of all interfaces configured in
     the system will be printed in the first column of output, even if no mul‐
     ticast group memberships are present on those interfaces.  The output may
     also be slightly different, as the kernel data structures are being tra‐
     versed with minimal post-processing of the output.

     When built without kvm(3) support, the information displayed by iiffmmccssttaatt
     is more limited.  This support is recommended for debugging purposes.  It
     requires super-user privilege if used to inspect a running kernel.

